WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.618
<v Program Manager>The director wants one number for the evening callback pilot.

00:00:03.618 --> 00:00:07.237
<v Program Manager>Callbacks completed went from 40 to 110 in a month.

00:00:07.237 --> 00:00:09.408
<v Program Manager>That is the story, isn't it?

00:00:09.758 --> 00:00:12.484
<v Data Analyst>It is part of the story. That number tells you

00:00:12.484 --> 00:00:15.211
<v Data Analyst>staff made more calls. It does not tell you whether

00:00:15.211 --> 00:00:16.574
<v Data Analyst>callers got what they needed.

00:00:16.924 --> 00:00:20.144
<v Program Manager>If they called back, they got help. What else would

00:00:20.144 --> 00:00:20.788
<v Program Manager>they need?

00:00:21.138 --> 00:00:24.927
<v Data Analyst>About 45% of them called again within three days. That

00:00:24.927 --> 00:00:28.715
<v Data Analyst>suggests many evening contacts did not solve the problem. Staff

00:00:28.715 --> 00:00:31.746
<v Data Analyst>say they could not mail forms after hours.

00:00:32.096 --> 00:00:35.436
<v Program Manager>So the count went up partly because people had to

00:00:35.436 --> 00:00:38.776
<v Program Manager>call twice. That changes things. But I still need something

00:00:38.776 --> 00:00:40.112
<v Program Manager>simple for the director.

00:00:40.462 --> 00:00:43.822
<v Data Analyst>Then give two numbers side by side. Callbacks completed, and

00:00:43.822 --> 00:00:47.182
<v Data Analyst>the share of callers who could take their next step.

00:00:47.182 --> 00:00:50.542
<v Data Analyst>When those two move together, you have real progress. When

00:00:50.542 --> 00:00:52.558
<v Data Analyst>they split, you have a question.

00:00:52.908 --> 00:00:57.033
<v Program Manager>The director also wants a target for staff reviews. Ninety

00:00:57.033 --> 00:00:59.508
<v Program Manager>percent answered within one business day.

00:00:59.858 --> 00:01:03.503
<v Data Analyst>Research on English ambulance services is worth knowing here. When

00:01:03.503 --> 00:01:07.149
<v Data Analyst>an eight-minute target carried public ratings, services did improve. But

00:01:07.149 --> 00:01:10.794
<v Data Analyst>about a third also corrected some response times, and some

00:01:10.794 --> 00:01:13.346
<v Data Analyst>showed a spike right at eight minutes.

00:01:13.696 --> 00:01:16.729
<v Program Manager>The staff on this team are not going to falsify

00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:17.032
<v Program Manager>data.

00:01:17.382 --> 00:01:20.792
<v Data Analyst>Most people in that study were not dishonest either. The

00:01:20.792 --> 00:01:24.202
<v Data Analyst>pressure works through small choices, like when the clock starts

00:01:24.202 --> 00:01:27.611
<v Data Analyst>or how a request is classed. And a different target,

00:01:27.611 --> 00:01:31.021
<v Data Analyst>for emergency waits, improved care with no sign of distortion.

00:01:31.021 --> 00:01:34.431
<v Data Analyst>The difference is often whether the number can move without

00:01:34.431 --> 00:01:35.454
<v Data Analyst>the work changing.

00:01:35.804 --> 00:01:39.349
<v Program Manager>So I keep the timeliness goal, pair it with the

00:01:39.349 --> 00:01:42.895
<v Program Manager>next-step measure, and review it as a team before anyone's

00:01:42.895 --> 00:01:43.604
<v Program Manager>individual review.

00:01:43.954 --> 00:01:47.065
<v Data Analyst>And look at the edges. If requests cluster just under

00:01:47.065 --> 00:01:50.176
<v Data Analyst>one day, look closer. That is where a target tells

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:52.042
<v Data Analyst>you what it is really measuring.
